I want you to take a moment and picture  the place where you feel most alive the  place where you feel energized happiest  maybe in the zone take a moment to look  around your happy place  and notice what you see what you smell  what you hear for me my happy place is a  grassy field in New Hampshire riding my  horse Brutus the Sun is warm and the air  is fresh and I take a deep breath and  feel alert and energized but also  relaxed I've asked a lot of people about  their own happy places and what I found  is that people prefer all sorts of  different places some like it loud  others like it quiet some prefer to be  inside while others love to be  surrounded by nature but what I have  found is that no one's happy place looks  like this the traditional office today  I want to share with you how we can  build happier healthier more productive  spaces for people it's a concept that  I've described to many of you as  wellness real estate the truth is we  spend a lot of our time indoors  in fact as Americans we spend over 90  percent of our lives inside that means  that the built environment can have a  drastic impact on our health and  well-being as future business leaders I  want to provide you with the information  and tools you need to build better  organizations by understanding what  healthier spaces look and feel like in  an increasingly competitive market  there's never been a better time to  understand what truly impacts health and  productivity for the last five years  I've been working at companies that aim  to make buildings healthier the  opportunity for impact is massive as  health is the fastest-growing and  arguably most important market and real  estate is the world's largest asset  class most recently I've been working  with the well building standard which is  a building certification program that  aims to make  healthier through design operations and  also policies today I want to share with  you my favorite well concepts where I  see the greatest opportunity for impact  air and light let's start with air when  you think about poor air quality you  probably imagine people wearing  facemasks  walking around in smoggy environments  typically in developing cities or if you  were in California last year during the  fires you may picture yourself walking  outside and feeling the soot in the air  hit your nose and eyes but even if there  isn't smog and even if there aren't  fires  outdoor air quality can be fairly poor  and what's concerning is that indoor air  can actually be 2 to 5 times more  contaminant contaminated than outdoor  air and what's concerning about indoor  air is that many of the contaminants we  find inside are actually invisible in  2014 Harvard did a study that brought  this idea of healthy indoor air quality  and work together they did this by  studying common office workers doing  common office tasks and looked at how  they were responding in terms of crisis  response and decision making they showed  that by increasing the amount of fresh  air in a space and decreasing the amount  of contaminants in that space they could  improve outcomes by nearly 300 percent  how did they do this they started by  replacing furniture office supplies and  cleaning products with equivalent  products that didn't off gas as many  contaminants into the indoor environment  they also thought about how much fresh  air was coming through a space I'm sure  we can all picture ourselves sitting in  a stuffy conference room feeling  sleepier and sleepier or how many of you  can picture yourself in an airplane  waiting to take off feeling sleepy er  and even a little bit dizzy  both of these responses are a result of  too many people in a small space  breathing out carbon dioxide without  enough fresh oxygen coming through that  space luckily there's an easy solution  for this called demand controlled  ventilation  with demand controlled ventilation  sensors sit in a potentially densely  occupied room and as more people come  into that space and breathe out carbon  dioxide the building's mechanical system  automatically pushes more fresh air into  that space now there were all air  experts let's talk a little bit about  light with a show of hands how many of  you use night shift on your iPhone and  for those of you with your hands up keep  them up there how many of you know why  you use night shift on your iPhone all  right about half the room is an expert  already in this space perfect for those  of you with your hands up you know that  our eyes are for more than just seeing  our eyes or more specifically our optic  nerves are responsible for perceiving  the amount and color temperature of  light in any given space let's take a  step back in time to think about why  this is back to a time before  electricity when we relied on the Sun  for most of our life in the morning as  the Sun rose we saw bright white blue  tones of light telling our bodies to  wake up and be alert and in the  afternoon and evening as the Sun set we  saw warmer tones of light or golden  tones telling our bodies to relax and  start preparing for sleep this 24-hour  sleep-wake cycle is known as our  circadian rhythm and it determines many  of our bodily functions so what happens  when we disrupt that cycle for example  when we see bright white blue iPhone  light right before we go to sleep well  we're telling our bodies to wake up and  be alert instead of actually going to  sleep and we wonder why we have so many  sleep disorders and what happens when we  sit under the same office lighting all  day long well in the US and in many  cities across the world typical office  lighting is warm in color temperature so  it's actually telling us to prepare for  sleep all day long  luckily there are easy solutions for  this as well having access to natural  daylight can keep our circadian rhythms  in line and even we don't have access to  natural daylight we can think about the  design of our overhead lighting or even  add a task  to a workstation they can give us the  right amount in color temperature of  light throughout the day with this new  insight I want to take you to another  one of my happy places but first picture  my commute a bustling New York City  Street stepping off of a crowded subway  platform all of this busyness disappears  as I step in and out of the elevator  into day Lowe's headquarters my office  would always hits me first is the quiet  thanks to carefully designed acoustical  panels on the ceiling walls and floor  the space is nearly silent 15 active  green walls greet me when I walk in not  only adding greenery to the space but  they have special nodes on their roots  that are pulling contaminants out of the  indoor air my arrival has triggered the  lights to turn on right white blue  because it's morning but I know that  over the course of the day they'll fade  to a golden color temperature helping me  prepare for sleep I take a deep breath  of fresh air and I feel alert I smell a  little bit of nature thanks to the  wooden staircase that connects our two  floors I know the workday must begin but  it doesn't all seem so bad as I grab an  organic banana from the cafe and sit  down in my organ on eclis adjustable  chair  my mind is clear now you may be thinking  that we can't all work in an office like  Dale OHS but Dallas wasn't just designed  with its own employees in mind it was  designed as a demonstration space to  show people what it looks and feels like  to be in a space that was designed with  people in mind and other major players  like CBRE and even Microsoft are also  implementing wellness design you may  also be thinking that all of this seems  expensive but luckily it isn't more  expensive it's simply smarter design and  even where there are small increases in  cost there's a clear return on  investment the American Society of  interior designers did a study of their  own employees after moving to a  healthier space and found increases in  productivity of up to 16% as Future  Business Leaders we have the power to  make our employees healthier  we can build companies that are not only  more profitable but also better for our  people and while it's the right thing to  do it can also make us more competitive  we can benefit from increases in  productivity and improved attraction and  retention my hope is that someday our  buildings will actually make us better  than when we walked through that door  and maybe someday our offices will  remind us all a little more of our own  happy place thank you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

